                              \n                                        \n                                        ." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069573/state.json" "file_sha256" "02986b017e60b26f877f485b21894c19c2d1b4c9dfda849c634fa82d5492b19e"} {"question" "q-139426" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The entry ${p}_{ij}$ lies at the intersection of the $3$rd row and $2$nd column. Hence, we have $i=3$ and $j=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069573/state.json" "file_sha256" "02986b017e60b26f877f485b21894c19c2d1b4c9dfda849c634fa82d5492b19e"} {"question" "q-139426" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069573/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "71d2e80810698192903daaeb9b3f75e622d1729fc73843fd6baa84f505c9d288"} {"question" "q-139426" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069573/state.json" "file_sha256" "02986b017e60b26f877f485b21894c19c2d1b4c9dfda849c634fa82d5492b19e"} {"question" "q-139426" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069573/state.json" "file_sha256" "02986b017e60b26f877f485b21894c19c2d1b4c9dfda849c634fa82d5492b19e"} {"question" "q-139426" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069573/state.json" "file_sha256" "02986b017e60b26f877f485b21894c19c2d1b4c9dfda849c634fa82d5492b19e"} {"question" "q-139426" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069573/state.json" "file_sha256" "02986b017e60b26f877f485b21894c19c2d1b4c9dfda849c634fa82d5492b19e"} {"question" "q-139431" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Maria and John are playing rock, paper, scissors. In each round, the players pick one of those three objects, and the rules are that rock beats scissors, scissors beats paper, and paper beats rock. \n\nThey decide to extend the game and make it so that the winner of each round receives $$2$ from the loser. So, for example, if Maria picks scissors and John picks rock, then Maria pays $$2$ to John.\n\nMaria represents the money she could earn or lose after one game using the following table.\n\n|  |  | John |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Rock** | **Paper** | **Scissors** |\n| Maria | **Rock** | $0$ | $-2$ | $2$ |\n| **Paper** | $2$ | $0$ | $-2$ |  |\n| **Scissors** | $-2$ | $2$ | $0$ |  |\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nMaria represents the information in the table using the following matrix:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The dimensions of $P$ are$\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\times $\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIn index notation, the highlighted entry of the matrix $P$ above is ${p}_{ij},$ where$\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $i=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         and \n$j=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nCurrent part:\n\nMaria and John agreed to increase the bet. After increasing, Maria's payoff matrix became\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis new matrix is obtained from matrix $P$ using the scalar multiplication $kP$, where\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,k=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069573/state.json" "file_sha256" "02986b017e60b26f877f485b21894c19c2d1b4c9dfda849c634fa82d5492b19e"} {"question" "q-139431" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To express $kP,$ we multiply each entry of $P$ by $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kP & =k[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2k & 2k \\\\ 2k & 0 & -2k \\\\ -2k & 2k & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the above result equal to the value given for $kP$ in the problem statement:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2k & 2k \\\\ 2k & 0 & -2k \\\\ -2k & 2k & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by equating any two corresponding (nonzero) elements. Let's choose the top-right elements, $2k$ and $6.$ Equating these elements, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k & =6 \\\\ k & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We would get the same result by equating any two corresponding (nonzero) elements." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069573/state.json" "file_sha256" "02986b017e60b26f877f485b21894c19c2d1b4c9dfda849c634fa82d5492b19e"} {"question" "q-139431" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069573/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "71d2e80810698192903daaeb9b3f75e622d1729fc73843fd6baa84f505c9d288"} {"question" "q-139431" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069573/state.json" "file_sha256" "02986b017e60b26f877f485b21894c19c2d1b4c9dfda849c634fa82d5492b19e"} {"question" "q-139431" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069573/state.json" "file_sha256" "02986b017e60b26f877f485b21894c19c2d1b4c9dfda849c634fa82d5492b19e"} {"question" "q-139440" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Maria and John are playing rock, paper, scissors. In each round, the players pick one of those three objects, and the rules are that rock beats scissors, scissors beats paper, and paper beats rock. \n\nThey decide to extend the game and make it so that the winner of each round receives $$2$ from the loser. So, for example, if Maria picks scissors and John picks rock, then Maria pays $$2$ to John.\n\nMaria represents the money she could earn or lose after one game using the following table.\n\n|  |  | John |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Rock** | **Paper** | **Scissors** |\n| Maria | **Rock** | $0$ | $-2$ | $2$ |\n| **Paper** | $2$ | $0$ | $-2$ |  |\n| **Scissors** | $-2$ | $2$ | $0$ |  |\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nMaria represents the information in the table using the following matrix:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The dimensions of $P$ are$\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\times $\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIn index notation, the highlighted entry of the matrix $P$ above is ${p}_{ij},$ where$\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $i=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         and \n$j=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nMaria and John agreed to increase the bet. After increasing, Maria's payoff matrix became\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis new matrix is obtained from matrix $P$ using the scalar multiplication $kP$, where\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,k=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nCurrent part:\n\nThey then agreed that both would put two dollars into a saving box in case of a draw. Hence, the payoff matrix for Maria is now \n\n$$\nP-2{I}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -2 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 6 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSuppose that each outcome in the game is equally likely. Let $X$ be the profit Maria can earn after playing the game once with the payoff matrix above. The probability mass function for $X$ is given below.\n\n| $X$ | $-6$ | $-2$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(X)$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $q$ | $r$ |\n\nFind the values of $q$ and $r.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $q=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n$\\,r=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069573/state.json" "file_sha256" "02986b017e60b26f877f485b21894c19c2d1b4c9dfda849c634fa82d5492b19e"} {"question" "q-139440" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In each instance of the game, there are three possible outcomes for $X$:\n\n* The result $X=-6$ occurs $3$ times out of $9$ in the payoff matrix. Therefore the corresponding probability is $\\frac{3}{9}=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\n* The result $X=-2$ occurs $3$ times out of $9$ in the payoff matrix. Therefore the corresponding probability is $\\frac{3}{9}=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\n* The result $X=6$ occurs $3$ times out of $9$ in the payoff matrix. Therefore the corresponding probability is $\\frac{3}{9}=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nHence, the probability mass function for $X$ is\n\n| $X$ | $-6$ | $-2$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(X)$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n\nHence, $q=r=\\frac{1}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069573/state.json" "file_sha256" "02986b017e60b26f877f485b21894c19c2d1b4c9dfda849c634fa82d5492b19e"} {"question" "q-139440" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069573/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "71d2e80810698192903daaeb9b3f75e622d1729fc73843fd6baa84f505c9d288"} {"question" "q-139440" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069573/state.json" "file_sha256" "02986b017e60b26f877f485b21894c19c2d1b4c9dfda849c634fa82d5492b19e"} {"question" "q-139440" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069573/state.json" "file_sha256" "02986b017e60b26f877f485b21894c19c2d1b4c9dfda849c634fa82d5492b19e"} {"question" "q-139440" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069573/state.json" "file_sha256" "02986b017e60b26f877f485b21894c19c2d1b4c9dfda849c634fa82d5492b19e"} {"question" "q-139440" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069573/state.json" "file_sha256" "02986b017e60b26f877f485b21894c19c2d1b4c9dfda849c634fa82d5492b19e"} {"question" 