          \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIn index notation, the highlighted entry of the matrix $P$ above is ${p}_{ij},$ where$\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $i=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         and \n$j=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nMaria and John agreed to increase the bet. After increasing, Maria's payoff matrix became\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis new matrix is obtained from matrix $P$ using the scalar multiplication $kP$, where\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,k=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nThey then agreed that both would put two dollars into a saving box in case of a draw. Hence, the payoff matrix for Maria is now \n\n$$\nP-2{I}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -2 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 6 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSuppose that each outcome in the game is equally likely. Let $X$ be the profit Maria can earn after playing the game once with the payoff matrix above. The probability mass function for $X$ is given below.\n\n| $X$ | $-6$ | $-2$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(X)$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $q$ | $r$ |\n\nFind the values of $q$ and $r.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $q=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{1}{3}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n$\\,r=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{1}{3}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nThe probability $p$ that Maria will lose money on her next turn is $p=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069573/state.json" "file_sha256" "02986b017e60b26f877f485b21894c19c2d1b4c9dfda849c634fa82d5492b19e"} {"question" "q-139441" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We want to find the probability that $X=-6$ or $X=-2$:\n\nUsing the values in the table found in part 5, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\in {-6,-2}) & =P(X=-6)+P(X=-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069573/state.json" "file_sha256" "02986b017e60b26f877f485b21894c19c2d1b4c9dfda849c634fa82d5492b19e"} {"question" "q-139441" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069573/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "71d2e80810698192903daaeb9b3f75e622d1729fc73843fd6baa84f505c9d288"} {"question" "q-139441" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069573/state.json" "file_sha256" "02986b017e60b26f877f485b21894c19c2d1b4c9dfda849c634fa82d5492b19e"} {"question" "q-139441" "field" "field-1" "attribu