n\nThis new matrix is obtained from matrix $P$ using the scalar multiplication $kP$, where\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,k=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nThey then agreed that both would put two dollars into a saving box in case of a draw. Hence, the payoff matrix for Maria is now \n\n$$\nP-2{I}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -2 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 6 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSuppose that each outcome in the game is equally likely. Let $X$ be the profit Maria can earn after playing the game once with the payoff matrix above. The probability mass function for $X$ is given below.\n\n| $X$ | $-6$ | $-2$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(X)$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $q$ | $r$ |\n\nFind the values of $q$ and $r.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $q=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{1}{3}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n$\\,r=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{1}{3}$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nThe probability $p$ that Maria will lose money on her next turn is $p=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{2}{3}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nCurrent part:\n\nExpressing the answer as a fraction, Maria's expected profit on her next turn is$\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         dollars." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069573/state.json" "file_sha256" "02986b017e60b26f877f485b21894c19c2d1b4c9dfda849c634fa82d5492b19e"} {"question" "q-139445" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From part 5, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n| $f(X)$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X$ | $-6$ | $-2$ | $6$ |\n\nThe expected value of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =-6\\cdot f(-6)+(-2)\\cdot f(-2)+6\\cdot f(6) \\\\  & =-6(\\frac{1}{3})+(-2)(\\frac{1}{3})+6(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =-2-\\frac{2}{3}+2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $-\\frac{2}{3}$ dollars." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069573/state.json" "file_sha256" "02986b017e60b26f877f485b21894c19c2d1b4c9dfda849c634fa82d5492b19e"} {"question" "q-139445" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069573/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "71d2e80810698192903daaeb9b3f75e622d1729fc73843fd6baa84f505c9d288"} {"question" "q-139445" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069573/state.json" "file_sha256" "02986b017e60b26f877f485b21894c19c2d1b4c9dfda849c634fa82d5492b19e"} {"question" "q-139445" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{2}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069573/state.json" "file_sha256" "02986b017e60b26f877f485b21894c19c2d1b4c9dfda849c634fa82d5492b19e"} {"question" "q-139453" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Maria and John are playing rock, paper, scissors. In each round, the players pick one of those three objects, and the rules are that rock beats scissors, scissors beats paper, and paper beats rock. \n\nThey decide to extend the game and make it so that the winner of each round receives $$2$ from the loser. So, for example, if Maria picks scissors and John picks rock, then Maria pays $$2$ to John.\n\nMaria represents the money she could earn or lose after one game using the following table.\n\n|  |  | John |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Rock** | **Paper** | **Scissors** |\n| Maria | **Rock** | $0$ | $-2$ | $2$ |\n| **Paper** | $2$ | $0$ | $-2$ |  |\n| **Scissors** | $-2$ | $2$ | $0$ |  |\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nMaria represents the information in the table using the following matrix:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The dimensions of $P$ are$\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\times $\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIn index notation, the highlighted entry of the matrix $P$ above is ${p}_{ij},$ where$\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $i=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         and \n$j=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nMaria and John agreed to increase the bet. After increasing, Maria's payoff matrix became\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis new matrix is obtained from matrix $P$ using the scalar multiplication $kP$, where\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,k=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nThey then agreed that both would put two dollars into a saving box in case of a draw. Hence, the payoff matrix for Maria is now \n\n$$\nP-2{I}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -2 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 6 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSuppose that each outcome in the game is equally likely. Let $X$ be the profit Maria can earn after playing the game once with the payoff matrix above. The probability mass function for $X$ is given below.\n\n| $X$ | $-6$ | $-2$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(X)$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $q$ | $r$ |\n\nFind the values of $q$ and $r.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $q=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{1}{3}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n$\\,r=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{1}{3}$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nThe probability $p$ that Maria will lose money on her next turn is $p=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{2}{3}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nExpressing the answer as a fraction, Maria's expected profit on her next turn is$\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{2}{3}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         dollars.\n\nCurrent part:\n\nThe money in the saving box accumulates until the very last game. Rounded to three decimal places, the probability that the saving box contains exactly $$20$ after $10$ games is$\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069573/state.json" "file_sha256" "02986b017e60b26f877f485b21894c19c2d1b4c9dfda849c634fa82d5492b19e"} {"question" "q-139453" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In each drawn game, both players deposit $$2$ each, or $$4$ in total, into the saving box with probability $\\frac{1}{3}.$ \n\nHence, the saving box will contain $$20$ dollars if Maria and John draw $5$ games.\n\nThen, if $Y$ is the number of times a game is drawn out of $10$ possible games, then \n\n$$\nY∼B(10,\\frac{1}{3}).\n$$\n\nSo, $Y$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(y)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{y}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{y}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{10-y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the desired probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y=5) & =f(5) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{5}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{5}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{10-5} \\\\  & =252{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{5}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{5} \\\\  & =0.137,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 